t, handle admonitions and footnotes with regex, then convert to HTML with marked.\n\nLet me think about the implementation more carefully.\n\nFor footnotes:\n1. Find `[^label]: text` definitions at the bottom, collect them\n2. Replace `[^label]` references in the text with `<sup><a href=\"#fn-N\">N</a></sup>` where N is the index\n3. Append footnote section at the end\n\nFor admonitions:\n1. Find `:::(note|warning|tip)\\n...\\n:::` blocks\n2. Replace with `<div class=\"admonition admonition-$1\">...</div>`\n\nFor markdown, convert with `marked.parse()`.\n\nLet me also think about the order of processing. It probably makes sense to:\n1. Extract footnotes definitions\n2. Replace footnote references\n3. Convert admonitions\n4. Convert markdown to HTML\n5. Append footnotes section\n\nActually, I need to be careful. The admonitions are markdown-like but aren't standard. I should handle them before markdown conversion.\n\nWait, let me re-read the tests more carefully:\n\n```\nit('converts :::note container to admonition div', async () => {\n  const file = makeFile({ content: ':::note\\nThis is a note.\\n:::' });\n  const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n  expect(result.content).toContain('<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">');\n  expect(result.content).toContain('This is a note.');\n});\n```\n\nSo `:::note\\nThis is a note.\\n:::` should become `<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">\\nThis is a note.\\n</div>` and then the markdown parser would wrap the paragraph text.\n\nActually wait - I think the admonition content could contain markdown that should also be converted. Let me think about the order again.\n\nHmm, actually the simplest approach might be to:\n1. Extract footnote definitions \n2. Replace footnote references\n3. Convert admonition blocks to HTML divs \n4. Run the rest through marked.parse()\n5. Append footnotes section\n\nLet me also look at the content more carefully. For admonitions, the content inside could be markdown. So I should convert that to HTML too... but the test just checks that \"This is a note.\" is present. Let me keep it simple and just wrap in the div, then let marked handle the rest.\n\nActually, I think if I convert `:::note\\nThis is a note.\\n:::` to some recognizable HTML first, and then run marked on the result, marked will preserve the HTML. That's the simplest approach.\n\nLet me code this up.\n\nFor the footnote regex, I need to be careful. The input is:\n```\nSome text[^1] here.\n\n[^1]: This is the footnote.\n```\n\nExpected output contains:\n- `<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>`\n- `<section class=\"footnotes\">`\n- `<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>`\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Match `[^1]: ...` patterns (footnote definitions at the bottom)\n2. Replace `[^1]` in the text (not at line start, not followed by `:`)\n3. Generate the footnote section\n\nLet me now write all the plugins. I'll create them one by one.\n\nActually, for the search-index plugin, I need to strip HTML tags. I can use a simple regex like `/<[^>]*>/g` to strip HTML.\n\nFor the sitemap plugin, I need to scan `ctx.output` keys and generate URLs. I also need to check for tag pages (from `taxonomy-tags` data) and feed.xml.\n\nFor the dev-server plugin, I need to generate a config object based on existing outputs and data.\n\nLet me also think about the express dependency - it's listed but I don't think I need it for the plugin itself; the plugin just generates config.\n\nNow let me implement all 8 plugins.\n\nLet me think about each one more carefully:\n\n### markdown-ext\n\n```typescript\nimport { Plugin, ContentFile, BuildContext } from '../core/types.js';\nimport { marked } from 'marked';\n\nexport function createMarkdownExtPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'markdown-ext',\n    version: '1.0.0',\n    dependencies: [],\n    hooks: {\n      transformContent(file: ContentFile, _ctx: BuildContext): ContentFile {\n        let content = file.content;\n        \n        // Skip if already HTML or empty\n        if (!content || content.trim() === '') {\n          return { ...file, content: content || '' };\n        }\n        \n        // Extract footnote definitions\n        const footnotes: { id: string; text: string }[] = [];\n        const footnoteDefRegex = /^\\[(\\^[^\\]]+)\\]:\\s*(.*)$/gm;\n        let match;\n        while ((match = footnoteDefRegex.exec(content)) !== null) {\n          footnotes.push({ id: match[1], text: match[2] });\n        }\n        \n        // Remove footnote definitions from content\n        content = content.replace(footnoteDefRegex, '');\n        \n        // Replace footnote references with superscript links\n        let refIndex = 0;\n        content = content.replace(/\\[\\^([^\\]]+)\\]/g, (_, id) => {\n          const idx = footnotes.findIndex(f => f.id === id);\n          if (idx !== -1) {\n            return `<sup><a href=\"#fn-${idx + 1}\">${idx + 1}</a></sup>`;\n          }\n          return _; // keep as-is if not found\n        });\n        \n        // Convert admonitions: :::note, :::warning, :::tip\n        content = content.replace(\n          /:::(\\w+)\\n([\\s\\S]*?):::/g,\n          (_, type, inner) => `<div class=\"admonition admonition-${type}\">\\n${inner.trim()}\\n</div>`\n        );\n        \n        // Convert markdown to HTML\n        const html = marked.parse(content) as string;\n        \n        // Append footnotes section\n        if (footnotes.length > 0) {\n          const footnotesHtml = footnotes.map((fn, i) => \n            `<li id=\"fn-${i + 1}\">${fn.text}</li>`\n          ).join('\\n');\n          html += `\\n<section class=\"footnotes\">\\n<ol>\\n${footnotesHtml}\\n</ol>\\n</section>`;\n        }\n        \n        return { ...file, content: html };\n      }\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, there's a problem with the footnote regex. The test input has `[^1]: This is the footnote.` but I'm also matching `[^1]` in the text. Let me be more careful. The footnote reference `[^1]` appears in the text (not at line start, not followed by `:`). The definition is `[^1]: text`. \n\nActually, let me re-examine the input:\n```\nSome text[^1] here.\n\n[^1]: This is the footnote.\n```\n\nIf I remove definitions first with `^\\[\\^[^\\]]+\\]:\\s*(.*)$` regex, then the remaining `[^1]` in the text is just a reference. That should work.\n\nBut wait, what if I remove definitions first, then the references won't have definitions? I need to capture the definitions before removing them.\n\nLet me restructure:\n1. Extract all footnote definitions (capture id and text)\n2. Remove definitions from content\n3. Replace all `[^id]` references in remaining content with superscript links\n4. If no definition found for a reference, keep it as-is\n\nFor numbering, I'll use 1-based index based on the order definitions appear.\n\nHmm, but the test uses `[^1]` and `[^2]` as IDs. Let me just use the numeric part of the ID for the href link. Actually, looking at the test again:\n\n```\nexpect(result.content).toContain('<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>');\nexpect(result.content).toContain('<section class=\"footnotes\">');\nexpect(result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>');\nexpect(result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-2\">Second note.</li>');\n```\n\nThe ID uses the same number as the reference. So `[^1]` → `#fn-1` and `[^2]` → `#fn-2`. Let me use the full ID as the anchor.\n\nActually wait, looking more carefully at `[^1]` - the test uses `[^1]` for reference and `[^1]:` for definition. So the ID is `1` (or `^1`). The anchor is `#fn-1` and the display is `1`.\n\nLet me extract the ID part (the `1` from `^1`) and use it.\n\n```typescript\nconst footnoteDefRegex = /^\\[(\\^\\d+)\\]:\\s*(.*)$/gm;\n```\n\nWait, the ID could also be a word. Let me keep it general: `^\\[(\\^[^\\]]+)\\]` - matches `[^anything]`.\n\nOK, let me re-evaluate:\n\nFor `[^1]: This is the footnote.` - I capture `^1` as the ID and `This is the footnote.` as the text.\nFor `[^1]` in te