plugin file. So I don't need a barrel export for the tests to work. But it's nice to have. Let me create one anyway.\n\nOK, let me now write all the plugins. I'm going to create the files in batches to be efficient.\n\nOne last thing: let me check the `marked` API one more time. In marked v17:\n- `marked.parse(markdownString)` - parse markdown to HTML\n- Options can be passed as second argument\n\nI should handle the case where `marked.parse()` returns a Promise by using `await`.\n\nOK I'll also need to double-check something about the markdown-ext tests. Let me re-read:\n\n```javascript\nit('converts basic markdown to HTML', async () => {\n  const file = makeFile({ content: '# Hello\\n\\nThis is a **bold** paragraph.' });\n  const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n  expect(result.content).toContain('<h1>');\n  expect(result.content).toContain('Hello');\n  expect(result.content).toContain('<strong>bold</strong>');\n});\n```\n\n`# Hello` in markdown produces `<h1>Hello</h1>`. And `**bold**` produces `<strong>bold</strong>`. Standard marked behavior.\n\n```javascript\nit('handles footnotes with [^1] syntax', async () => {\n  const file = makeFile({ content: 'Some text[^1] here.\\n\\n[^1]: This is the footnote.' });\n  const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n  expect(result.content).toContain('<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>');\n});\n```\n\nSo `[^1]` in text → `<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>`. And footnote definition `[^1]: This is the footnote.` becomes footnote content.\n\n```javascript\nit('renders footnote section at bottom of content', async () => {\n  const file = makeFile({ content: 'Text[^1] and more[^2].\\n\\n[^1]: First note.\\n[^2]: Second note.' });\n  const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n  expect(result.content).toContain('<section class=\"footnotes\">');\n  expect(result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>');\n  expect(result.content).to